{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015nS8GMf9Efb6ZoL3b5H1Fh","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# TypeScript\n2\t\n3\tApplies to `src/`, `test/`, and `scripts/`. Notes for one area are in\n4\tthat area's file (index in `AGENTS.md`).\n5\t\n6\t## Tooling\n7\t\n8\t- `npm test` builds `dist/cli.js`, then bundles and runs the tapzero\n9\t  suite; it does not typecheck. A new test module must be imported from\n10\t  `test/index.ts`. esbuild drops unused imports without resolving them,\n11\t  so only `npm run typecheck` catches a broken import.\n12\t- `npm run test:one -- test/<area>/<file>.ts` bundles one test module\n13\t  to `test/one-bundle.js` and runs it through tap-spec. Test modules\n14\t  cannot run directly under Node: their `.js` import specifiers only\n15\t  resolve once bundled. It does not typecheck; run `typecheck` too.\n16\t- Lint with `npm run lint`: it preloads `scripts/eslint-ts6.ts`, which\n17\t  gives eslint the TypeScript 6 API because typescript-eslint throws on\n18\t  the TypeScript 7 that `typecheck` uses.\n19\t- Node runs `scripts/` directly (type stripping), so they use only\n20\t  erasable syntax: no enums, namespaces, or parameter properties.\n21\t- `baseOptions` in `scripts/esbuild-options.ts` holds the esbuild\n22\t  options every bundle shares (text loaders for `.tmpl`, `.md`, `.sh`;\n23\t  `mainFields: ['module', 'main']`, which `jsonc-parser`'s UMD build\n24\t  needs); `scripts/bundle.ts` adds the `require` shim banner. A test\n25\t  that bundles code itself uses both.\n26\t- `npm run build` also writes `dist/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt`\n27\t  (`scripts/licenses.ts`) and fails, naming the package, when a bundled\n28\t  package lacks an allowlisted `license` field or a license file. A new\n29\t  runtime library must pass it; extending `ALLOWED_LICENSES` needs\n30\t  maintainer approval. `@substrate-system/debug` fails it today, so no\n31\t  `src/` module may import it yet. Runtime libraries are\n32\t  `devDependencies` (bundled); `dependencies` stays empty.\n33\t\n34\t## Source conventions\n35\t\n36\t- Keep source ASCII: write `\\uXXXX` escapes. Some editing tools decode\n37\t  escapes into literal characters when writing; check with\n38\t  `grep -nP '[^\\x00-\\x7f]'` after writing a file. eslint `--fix`\n39\t  (quote-props) rewrites a quoted `'\\u00e9'` object key into a literal\n40\t  non-ASCII identifier; put such keys in a const and use a computed key.\n41\t- eslint rejects control characters in regex literals\n42\t  (`no-control-regex`) and labeled statements (`no-labels`); scan char\n43\t  codes and use helper functions instead.\n44\t- Startup guards live in `src/startup.ts`, not `src/cli.ts`: importing\n45\t  the entry runs the CLI, so testable helpers sit in other modules.\n46\t- Module-level swappable functions use a mutable hooks object plus a\n47\t  delegating export (`terminalHooks`, `skillHooks.lookPath`), because\n48\t  tests cannot reassign an ESM `let` export. Swap the object's field\n49\t  and restore it in `t.cleanup`.\n50\t- Shared helpers; import these, never re-implement:\n51\t  `src/gocompat/` (`strings.ts`: Go TrimSpace/Ext/`%.2f`/strconv.Quote/\n52\t  byte-order sort and `truncateUtf8`; `duration.ts`: Go durations as\n53\t  bigint nanoseconds; `match.ts`: Go `path.Match`),\n54\t  `src/textsafe/textsafe.ts`, `src/fsutil/atomic.ts`\n55\t  (`writeFileAtomic`, options cover trust, credentials, and gate temp\n56\t  names and error messages), `src/git/git.ts` (the only git runner;\n57\t  pass the CLI context's `env`, it reads `process.env` only when none is\n58\t  given), `src/util/pool.ts` (`mapPool`).\n59\t\n60\t## CLI core (`src/cli/`)\n61\t\n62\t- Commands are `CommandDef`s (`command.ts`) listed in `commands.ts`;\n63\t  `run.ts` builds the yargs tree from that list plus `registerCommand`\n64\t  (tests), and calls the handler only after parsing succeeds.\n65\t- A handler gets a `RunContext` (cwd, env, signal, fetch, one shared\n66\t  `LineReader`) and throws `UsageError` / `ExitSignal` / any error; it\n67\t  never writes the error itself or reads process globals.\n68\t- A group with `subcommands` and no handler prints its help.\n69\t- yargs number parsing is off (`parse-numbers: false`). Declare an\n70\t  integer flag as `type:'string'` and parse decimal digits in the\n71\t  handler, throwing `UsageError` on a bad value (see `parseLimit` in\n72\t  `runs.ts`).\n73\t- Terminal detection and browser opening are swapped through\n74\t  `terminalHooks` (`terminal.ts`).\n75\t- With only the default `$0` command registered, yargs omits the\n76\t  Commands section from root help; it appears once a second command\n77\t  registers.\n78\t\n79\t## Review command (`src/cli/review.ts`)\n80\t\n81\t- End-to-end review tests use `settingsRepo` (`test/helpers/review.ts`):\n82\t  a temp repo with an uncommitted change, a config on a fake `chat`\n83\t  server, keys in env, `review.snapshot: false`, and approval via\n84\t  `roast trust --yes`. It chdirs into the repo.\n85\t- yargs keeps only the last occurrence of an array flag under\n86\t  `duplicate-arguments-array: false`; review slice flags are re-read\n87\t  from `ctx.argv` (`review-flags.ts`). Flags whose \"changed\" state\n88\t  matters have no yargs default (`undefined` = not given).\n89\t- The structured finder call offers `findings` plus the agent's\n90\t  `__roast_continue`; assert tool presence, not exact lists.\n91\t- Verification (step 17) runs only with `doVerify` and at least one\n92\t  finding. `verify` (`src/verify/verify.ts`) never throws; an abort\n93\t  becomes undecided verdicts, so the run still writes its record and\n94\t  exits 4. Its result types `notes` as `string[]|null` (Go parity), so\n95\t  CLI code after step 17 takes `VerifiedResult`, not `Result`.\n96\t- Verifier CLI tests (`test/cli/review-verify.ts`) put the verifier on\n97\t  its own profile and model and tell its requests apart by the\n98\t  `submit_verdict` tool. The default role key variable\n99\t  (`ROAST_VERIFIER_API_KEY`) backs any profile, so an invalid-key test\n100\t  must delete it from the env.\n101\t- The entry exits within the tick of the first SIGINT, and identical\n102\t  signals sent back to back coalesce, so a second-signal test needs a\n103\t  slow wind-down (a fake `git` that exits slowly on SIGTERM).\n104\t\n105\t## Tests\n106\t\n107\t- Use the `test` wrapper in `test/helpers/test.ts`, which adds Go-style\n108\t  `t.cleanup` (tapzero has no teardown hook). tapzero's `Test` has no\n109\t  `notOk`; write `t.ok(!x, ...)`.\n110\t- CLI tests run in-process with `runRoast` (`test/helpers/run.ts`),\n111\t  which makes a temp repo with an isolated env (`test/helpers/repo.ts`:\n112\t  `tempRepo`, `tempDir`, which also sets `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`).\n113\t  `test/cli/process.ts` and `test/tooling/bundle.ts` spawn `dist/cli.js`,\n114\t  so they need a fresh `npm run build` (`npm test` does that).\n115\t- Test environments come from `isolatedEnv`, which starts from\n116\t  `cleanEnv(process.env)` (`test/helpers/env.ts`): every `ROAST_*`,\n117\t  `OPENAI_*`, `OPENROUTER_*`, and `ANTHROPIC_*` variable is stripped. A\n118\t  new provider whose key variable has another prefix goes in its\n119\t  `AMBIENT_PREFIXES`. Tests set credentials in `io.env`, never in\n120\t  `process.env`.\n121\t- A review test needs an approved `.roast.json` on the fake server:\n122\t  `settingsRepo` approves, or call `approveProjectConfig`. A test\n123\t  asserting a config error approves a valid config and then edits it,\n124\t  so a trust failure cannot mask the error. A doctor test that probes a\n125\t  custom endpoint approves in its temp repo (`approveDoctorConfig`,\n126\t  `test/cli/doctor.ts`), never in this checkout.\n127\t- Untracked files are part of a review's diff, so a test that checks\n128\t  which file's text reached the fake server keeps decoys out of scope\n129\t  (for example through `.git/info/exclude`).\n130\t- Fakes: `startInferenceServer` (`test/helpers/inference-server.ts`)\n131\t  speaks the three protocols; `providerServer`\n132\t  (`test/helpers/provider-server.ts`) adds key-check and OpenRouter\n133\t  routes. `runRoast({ redirectTo })` uses `redirectingFetch`, so a\n134\t  profile on a built-in endpoint reaches the fake. `requestRecorder` and\n135\t  `reviewMeta` (`test/helpers/review.ts`) read what the fake received\n136\t  and the run's `meta.json`.\n137\t- Swap terminals and the browser with `simulateTerminals` /\n138\t  `captureBrowser` (`test/helpers/terminal.ts`).\n139\t- Tests that spawn processes use `test/helpers/spawn.ts` (`ROOT`,\n140\t  `run`).\n141\t- Git runner tests observe a child by putting a fake `git` shell script\n142\t  first on the injected `PATH` (spawn resolves the command through\n143\t  `env.PATH`); see `test/git/git.ts`.\n144\t- Live tests live under `test/live/` (entry `index.ts`,\n145\t  `npm run test:live`); each passes with a \"skipped\" note when its\n146\t  `ROAST_LIVE_*` key is unset, so the TAP stream is never empty.\n147\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28032774-3a15-4d3b-9c60-8fc3e97c0e06","uuid":"2c22577c-28d5-4d34-a416-795fc4087248","timestamp":"2026-11-24T23:37:32.140Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/roast.review-fix/docs/agents/typescript.md","content":"# TypeScript\n\nApplies to `src/`, `test/`, and `scripts/`. Notes for one area are in\nthat area's file (index in `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Tooling\n\n- `npm test` builds `dist/cli.js`, then bundles and runs the tapzero\n  suite; it does not typecheck. A new test module must be imported from\n  `test/index.ts`. esbuild drops unused imports without resolving them,\n  so only `npm run typecheck` catches a broken import.\n- `npm run test:one -- test/<area>/<file>.ts` bundles one test module\n  to `test/one-bundle.js` and runs it through tap-spec. Test modules\n  cannot run directly under Node: their `.js` import specifiers only\n  resolve once bundled. It does not typecheck; run `typecheck` too.\n- Lint with `npm run lint`: it preloads `scripts/eslint-ts6.ts`, which\n  gives eslint the TypeScript 6 API because typescript-eslint throws on\n  the TypeScript 7 that `typecheck` uses.\n- Node runs `scripts/` directly (type stripping), so they use only\n  erasable syntax: no enums, namespaces, or parameter properties.\n- `baseOptions` in `scripts/esbuild-options.ts` holds the esbuild\n  options every bundle shares (text loaders for `.tmpl`, `.md`, `.sh`;\n  `mainFields: ['module', 'main']`, which `jsonc-parser`'s UMD build\n  needs); `scripts/bundle.ts` adds the `require` shim banner. A test\n  that bundles code itself uses both.\n- `npm run build` also writes `dist/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt`\n  (`scripts/licenses.ts`) and fails, naming the package, when a bundled\n  package lacks an allowlisted `license` field or a license file. A new\n  runtime library must pass it; extending `ALLOWED_LICENSES` needs\n  maintainer approval. `@substrate-system/debug` fails it today, so no\n  `src/` module may import it yet. Runtime libraries are\n  `devDependencies` (bundled); `dependencies` stays empty.\n\n## Source conventions\n\n- Keep source ASCII: write `\\uXXXX` escapes. Some editing tools decode\n  escapes into literal characters when writing; check with\n  `grep -nP '[^\\x00-\\x7f]'` after writing a file. eslint `--fix`\n  (quote-props) rewrites a quoted `'\\u00e9'` object key into a literal\n  non-ASCII identifier; put such keys in a const and use a computed key.\n- eslint rejects control characters in regex literals\n  (`no-control-regex`) and labeled statements (`no-labels`); scan char\n  codes and use helper functions instead.\n- Startup guards live in `src/startup.ts`, not `src/cli.ts`: importing\n  the entry runs the CLI, so testable helpers sit in other modules.\n- Module-level swappable functions use a mutable hooks object plus a\n  delegating export (`terminalHooks`, `skillHooks.lookPath`), because\n  tests cannot reassign an ESM `let` export. Swap the object's field\n  and restore it in `t.cleanup`.\n- Shared helpers; import these, never re-implement:\n  `src/gocompat/` (`strings.ts`: Go TrimSpace/Ext/`%.2f`/strconv.Quote/\n  byte-order sort and `truncateUtf8`; `duration.ts`: Go durations as\n  bigint nanoseconds; `match.ts`: Go `path.Match`),\n  `src/textsafe/textsafe.ts`, `src/fsutil/atomic.ts`\n  (`writeFileAtomic`, options cover trust, credentials, and gate temp\n  names and error messages), `src/git/git.ts` (the only git runner;\n  pass the CLI context's `env`, it reads `process.env` only when none is\n  given), `src/util/pool.ts` (`mapPool`).\n\n## CLI core (`src/cli/`)\n\n- Commands are `CommandDef`s (`command.ts`) listed in `commands.ts`;\n  `run.ts` builds the yargs tree from that list plus `registerCommand`\n  (tests), and calls the handler only after parsing succeeds.\n- A handler gets a `RunContext` (cwd, env, signal, fetch, one shared\n  `LineReader`) and throws `UsageError` / `ExitSignal` / any error; it\n  never writes the error itself or reads process globals.\n- A group with `subcommands` and no handler prints its help.\n- yargs number parsing is off (`parse-numbers: false`). Declare an\n  integer flag as `type:'string'` and parse decimal digits in the\n  handler, throwing `UsageError` on a bad value (see `parseLimit` in\n  `runs.ts`).\n- Terminal detection and browser opening are swapped through\n  `terminalHooks` (`terminal.ts`).\n- With only the default `$0` command registered, yargs omits the\n  Commands section from root help; it appears once a second command\n  registers.\n\n## Review command (`src/cli/review.ts`)\n\n- End-to-end review tests use `settingsRepo` (`test/helpers/review.ts`):\n  a temp repo with an uncommitted change, a config on a fake `chat`\n  server, keys in env, `review.snapshot: false`, and approval via\n  `roast trust --yes`. It chdirs into the repo.\n- yargs keeps only the last occurrence of an array flag under\n  `duplicate-arguments-array: false`; review slice flags are re-read\n  from `ctx.argv` (`review-flags.ts`). Flags whose \"changed\" state\n  matters have no yargs default (`undefined` = not given).\n- The structured finder call offers `findings` plus the agent's\n  `__roast_continue`; assert tool presence, not exact lists.\n- Verification (step 17) runs only with `doVerify` and at least one\n  finding. `verify` (`src/verify/verify.ts`) never throws; an abort\n  becomes undecided verdicts, so the run still writes its record and\n  exits 4. Its result types `notes` as `string[]|null` (Go parity), so\n  CLI code after step 17 takes `VerifiedResult`, not `Result`.\n- Verifier CLI tests (`test/cli/review-verify.ts`) put the verifier on\n  its own profile and model and tell its requests apart by the\n  `submit_verdict` tool. The default role key variable\n  (`ROAST_VERIFIER_API_KEY`) backs any profile, so an invalid-key test\n  must delete it from the env.\n- The entry exits within the tick of the first SIGINT, and identical\n  signals sent back to back coalesce, so a second-signal test needs a\n  slow wind-down (a fake `git` that exits slowly on SIGTERM).\n\n## Tests\n\n- Use the `test` wrapper in `test/helpers/test.ts`, which adds Go-style\n  `t.cleanup` (tapzero has no teardown hook). tapzero's `Test` has no\n  `notOk`; write `t.ok(!x, ...)`.\n- CLI tests run in-process with `runRoast` (`test/helpers/run.ts`),\n  which makes a temp repo with an isolated env (`test/helpers/repo.ts`:\n  `tempRepo`, `tempDir`, which also sets `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`).\n  `test/cli/process.ts` and `test/tooling/bundle.ts` spawn `dist/cli.js`,\n  so they need a fresh `npm run build` (`npm test` does that).\n- Test environments come from `isolatedEnv`, which starts from\n  `cleanEnv(process.env)` (`test/helpers/env.ts`): every `ROAST_*`,\n  `OPENAI_*`, `OPENROUTER_*`, and `ANTHROPIC_*` variable is stripped. A\n  new provider whose key variable has another prefix goes in its\n  `AMBIENT_PREFIXES`. Tests set credentials in `io.env`, never in\n  `process.env`.\n- A review test needs an approved `.roast.json` on the fake server:\n  `settingsRepo` approves, or call `approveProjectConfig`. A test\n  asserting a config error approves a valid config and then edits it,\n  so a trust failure cannot mask the error. A doctor test that probes a\n  custom endpoint approves in its temp repo (`approveDoctorConfig`,\n  `test/cli/doctor.ts`), never in this checkout.\n- Untracked files are part of a review's diff, so a test that checks\n  which file's text reached the fake server keeps decoys out of scope\n  (for example through `.git/info/exclude`).\n- Fakes: `startInferenceServer` (`test/helpers/inference-server.ts`)\n  speaks the three protocols; `providerServer`\n  (`test/helpers/provider-server.ts`) adds key-check and OpenRouter\n  routes. `runRoast({ redirectTo })` uses `redirectingFetch`, so a\n  profile on a built-in endpoint reaches the fake. `requestRecorder` and\n  `reviewMeta` (`test/helpers/review.ts`) read what the fake received\n  and the run's `meta.json`.\n- Swap terminals and the browser with `simulateTerminals` /\n  `captureBrowser` (`test/helpers/terminal.ts`).\n- Tests that spawn processes use `test/helpers/spawn.ts` (`ROOT`,\n  `run`).\n- Git runner tests observe a child by putting a fake `git` shell script\n  first on the injected `PATH` (spawn resolves the command through\n  `env.PATH`); see `test/git/git.ts`.\n- Live tests live under `test/live/` (entry `index.ts`,\n  `npm run test:live`); each passes with a \"skipped\" note when its\n  `ROAST_LIVE_*` key is unset, so the TAP stream is never empty.\n","numLines":147,"startLine":1,"totalLines":147}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015nS8GMf9Efb6ZoL3b5H1Fh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
